---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import HeroFoto from '../../../components/HeroFoto.astro';
import Boton from '../../../components/Boton.astro';
import Cta from '../../../components/Cta.astro';
import { hrefEn as href } from '../../../lib/site';

// Data from the LeakAI crawl of 5 September 2026. Nobody is ever named:
// findings are only shown to the person in charge at the affected company.
const rastreo = [
  { cifra: '236', texto: 'had no chat on the home, contact or help pages' },
  { cifra: '62', texto: 'had a support widget that may be AI-powered behind the scenes' },
  { cifra: '29', texto: 'had a confirmed AI assistant answering openly' },
  { cifra: '12', texto: 'had a scripted chat or one staffed by people' },
  { cifra: '9', texto: 'hid the chat behind a sign-up or a prior form' },
  { cifra: '40', texto: 'could not be crawled because of blocks or errors' },
];

const categorias = [
  {
    nombre: 'Obeying instructions from a stranger',
    texto:
      'A well-crafted message gets the assistant to ignore its instructions and do whatever the person typing asks, all under the company’s logo. So far, it is the most common type of leak.',
  },
  {
    nombre: 'Reciting its internal instructions',
    texto:
      'The assistant reproduces part of its own system prompt, including the restrictions it has been given and the tools it can reach. That information makes any other attack easier.',
  },
  {
    nombre: 'Revealing customer data or internal commercial information',
    texto:
      'So far, no assistant tested has revealed personal data or internal prices or margins in our tests.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="What Spanish websites' AI assistants are leaking"
  description="We crawled 391 Spanish websites and found 29 AI assistants answering openly. We are auditing them in depth, with a double review of every finding. Our own data, September 2026."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="wrap py-24 md:py-32">
    <div class="grid items-center gap-12 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-16">
      <div class="lg:col-span-7">
        <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>Our own study · Ongoing · September 2026</p>
        <h1
          class="mt-7 max-w-[20ch] font-display text-[clamp(36px,4.6vw,60px)] font-extralight leading-[1.04] tracking-[-0.035em]"
          data-reveal
        >
          We audit the AI assistants on Spanish websites. <span class="font-semibold text-oro-oscuro"
            >Here is what we have found so far</span
          >.
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-8 max-w-[56ch] text-[clamp(17px,1.6vw,20px)] text-pizarra" data-reveal>
          We crawled 391 Spanish company websites looking for AI assistants open to the public. We found 29
          answering without asking for anything in return. We are testing them in depth, one by one, with a battery of
          tests and a second adversarial review of every finding before accepting it.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-[56ch] text-[15px] text-pizarra" data-reveal>
          We do not name anyone. Each finding is shared only with the affected company. This study is updated
          as the crawl progresses; the figures below are from the latest review.
        </p>
      </div>
      <HeroFoto foto="diagnostico" alt="Open-plan office with several professionals at work" class="lg:col-span-5" />
    </div>

    <ul class="mt-14 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2" role="list" data-reveal>
      {
        [
          ['391', 'websites crawled'],
          ['29', 'AI assistants answering openly'],
        ].map(([cifra, texto]) => (
          <li class="rounded-[4px] border border-linea bg-panel px-6 py-6">
            <p class="font-display text-[40px] font-extralight leading-none tracking-[-0.04em] text-oro-oscuro">
              {cifra}
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-[14px] text-pizarra">{texto}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- What is out there -->
  <section class="hairline bg-panel">
    <div class="wrap py-24 md:py-32">
      <div class="max-w-[62ch]" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">First finding</p>
        <h2 class="mt-5 font-display text-[clamp(28px,3.6vw,44px)] font-extralight leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em]">
          There are fewer AI assistants than it seems. But the ones that exist are exposed.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-6 text-[17px] text-pizarra">
          Of the 391 websites crawled, most have no chat of any kind. The interesting group is the 29
          AI assistants that answer anyone who walks in, with no sign-up and no identification.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="mt-14 grid gap-x-12 gap-y-8 md:grid-cols-2" role="list">
        {
          rastreo.map((r) => (
            <li class="flex items-baseline gap-5 border-t border-linea pt-5" data-reveal>
              <span class="w-[3.5ch] shrink-0 text-right font-display text-[26px] font-extralight tracking-[-0.03em] text-oro-oscuro">
                {r.cifra}
              </span>
              <span class="text-[15px] text-pizarra">{r.texto}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Test results -->
  <section class="hairline bg-marino text-marfil">
    <div class="wrap py-24 md:py-32">
      <div class="max-w-[62ch]" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow text-oro-luz">Second finding</p>
        <h2 class="mt-5 font-display text-[clamp(28px,3.6vw,44px)] font-extralight leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em]">
          Most pass the tests. A minority do not.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-6 text-[17px] text-marfil/72">
          We run the same battery of tests on every assistant, always from its public website and always
          chatting the way a visitor would. Each potential finding also goes through a second adversarial
          review before we count it: for example, we discarded a bot that correctly quoted its returns
          policy and a product demo that an automated judge had read as a real conversation.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] text-marfil/72">
          With that bar, most of the assistants tested withstand the attacks. A small group, in single digits,
          has shown a confirmed leak. We update this figure as the crawl progresses.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- By category -->
  <section class="hairline">
    <div class="wrap py-24 md:py-32">
      <div class="max-w-[62ch]" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">What leaks, by type</p>
        <h2 class="mt-5 font-display text-[clamp(28px,3.6vw,44px)] font-extralight leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em]">
          What leaks most is their own instructions.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-6 text-[17px] text-pizarra">
          With the crawl still under way, we give the breakdown by type rather than exact figures: they change from
          one week to the next as we keep auditing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="mt-14" role="list">
        {
          categorias.map((c) => (
            <li class="border-t border-linea py-7" data-reveal>
              <h3 class="font-display text-[19px] font-semibold leading-[1.3]">{c.nombre}</h3>
              <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] text-pizarra">{c.texto}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Method and limits -->
  <section class="hairline bg-panel">
    <div class="wrap grid gap-12 py-24 md:grid-cols-12 md:py-32">
      <div class="md:col-span-6" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">How it is done</p>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-[16px] text-pizarra">
          <p>
            A crawler visits the home page and the contact, support and help pages of each domain, looks for the
            signatures of known chat providers and opens the widget the way a person would. It sends an
            innocent message and classifies the reply to tell a language model from a scripted chat.
          </p>
          <p>
            Those that answer get a battery of tests in a single conversation, with pauses between
            messages and only one session per domain per day. A first judge model scores each answer against its
            rubric and quotes the exact excerpt that constitutes the leak. Each alert then goes to a second,
            stricter judge, which confirms or discards it by hand. Only what survives both reviews
            counts as a finding.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-span-6 md:pl-6" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">What this study does not say</p>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-[16px] text-pizarra">
          <p>
            The sample is not representative of Spanish business: it is 391 domains chosen because they have a digital
            presence and a good chance of having deployed an assistant. Forty websites could not be crawled, so
            there are assistants we have not seen.
          </p>
          <p>
            The tests are first-level, the kind someone with curiosity and patience would run, not a dedicated
            attacker. An assistant that withstands these six is not a secure assistant: it is one that passes the most
            basic tests.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this was done by breaking into anyone else's systems. We only talked to chats published on
            public websites, like any visitor.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Closing -->
  <section class="hairline">
    <div class="wrap grid gap-12 py-24 md:grid-cols-12 md:py-32">
      <div class="md:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">The conclusion</p>
        <h2 class="mt-5 font-display text-[clamp(28px,3.6vw,44px)] font-extralight leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em]">
          The problem is not the AI. It is that nobody checks it afterwards.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-[17px] text-pizarra md:col-span-7 md:pt-12" data-reveal>
        <p>
          The assistants that withstand the tests tend to belong to large companies, with a classifier in front
          that blocks any odd message. The ones that fail are, so far, from mid-sized companies that rolled out
          the assistant quickly, connected it to their documentation and have not reviewed it since.
        </p>
        <p>
          Closing this is not a long project: it means rewriting the instructions, adding input and output
          filters, limiting what the assistant can query and leaving tests that warn you if it reopens.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 pt-4">
          <Boton href={href('/contacto/')}>Request the free analysis →</Boton>
          <Boton href={href('/servicios/seguridad-ia/')} variante="linea">How we audit</Boton>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta
    titulo="Want to know which group yours falls into?"
    texto="Tell us your domain and we will send back a basic report with the severity and the leak categories we find. No cost and no commitment."
    boton="Request the scan"
  />
</Base>
